/* Conformance test for X.509 / CMS / PEM trust-gate entry points in librktcrypto.
 *
 * Targets (previously ZERO test coverage):
 *   rktcrypto_base64_decode, rktcrypto_pem_to_der,
 *   rktcrypto_x509_verify_selfsigned, rktcrypto_cms_verify
 *
 * Test material (DER/PEM/CMS/base64 vectors) is generated by the accompanying
 * python `cryptography` 49 script and embedded below. Build:
 *   cc -O2 -I. -o /tmp/tx asm/tests/test_x509_cms.c \
 *      ../build/cs/c/rktcrypto/librktcrypto.a
 *
 * SECURITY FINDING (this test currently reports FAILURES because of it):
 *   rktcrypto_cms_verify() REJECTS a *valid* RSA-2048/SHA-256 CMS SignedData
 *   that OpenSSL verifies as good ("cms_verify valid RSA/SHA256 SignedData"
 *   prints FAIL). Root cause: the RSA public-exponent deserialization in
 *   rktcrypto_x509.c,
 *       for(j=0;j<16;j++) eb[j]=(unsigned char)(ev>>(120-8*j));
 *   shifts a 64-bit uint64_t by up to 120 bits -- undefined behavior (shift
 *   count >= type width). On arm64 the count is masked mod 64, so the loop
 *   duplicates the exponent into limb 1 and bn_from_be() yields e with top=2,
 *   e = (65537<<64)|65537 instead of 65537. sig^e mod n is then wrong and the
 *   signature check fails. The buggy loop appears TWICE: in cert_rsa_pubkey()
 *   (used by cms_verify) and in rktcrypto_x509_verify_selfsigned(). Because it
 *   is UB, whether it manifests is compiler/codegen dependent: the shipped
 *   librktcrypto.a mis-verifies CMS but happens to still accept the self-signed
 *   RSA cert. Fix: build eb by zeroing then placing the elen exponent bytes at
 *   the low end (well-defined), e.g.
 *       for(j=0;j<16;j++) eb[j]=0;
 *       for(j=0;j<elen && j<16;j++) eb[15-j]=ep[elen-1-j];
 *   Verified: with that change cms_verify() returns 1 for this vector on every
 *   run. This test asserts the CORRECT behavior on purpose and must not be
 *   weakened to mask the defect.
 */
